; `sconcat` on two LISTS must concatenate them — not read their Cons cells as a pair.
;
; A v2 list is `Data("Cons", [head, tail])`: a two-field Data value, structurally a PAIR. The
; generators' `sconcat` had a "concatenate the fields of any two Data values" arm for tuples, and
; two Cons CELLS match it, so `List(1,2) ++ List(3)` came back as a `Tuple4` — which then failed far
; away, as `Tuple4.isEmpty was called but does not exist`. That is
; `corpus-contract-scljet-jdbc-v2-timeout`: four scljet corpus cases, red on the v2 lane, and the
; head of a chain of six defects.
;
; NO EXISTING FIXTURE USED `sconcat` AT ALL — the same hole `__autoOutput__` documents one file
; over. This harness compares every generator against the VM byte-for-byte and was green while the
; Rust generator returned a tuple for a list concat, because nothing here ever asked it to.
;
; The last two cases are ANTI-ROWS and matter as much as the first three: a list arm placed ahead of
; the Data++Data arm must not eat the behaviour that arm is FOR. String concat and tuple concat are
; both correct today and must stay correct.
;
; Expected output, from the VM:
;   List(1, 2, 3)
;   List(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)
;   List(7)
;   abcd
;   (1, 2, 3, 4)
(program
 (defs
  (def main
   (lam 0
    (seq
     ; two lists
     (prim __autoOutput__
      (prim sconcat
       (ctor Cons (lit (int 1)) (ctor Cons (lit (int 2)) (ctor Nil)))
       (ctor Cons (lit (int 3)) (ctor Nil))))
     (seq
      ; a three-way chain — the shape the corpus actually hit, where the LEFT operand of the
      ; outer concat is itself the result of a concat
      (prim __autoOutput__
       (prim sconcat
        (prim sconcat
         (ctor Cons (lit (int 1)) (ctor Cons (lit (int 2)) (ctor Nil)))
         (ctor Cons (lit (int 3)) (ctor Nil)))
        (ctor Cons (lit (int 4)) (ctor Cons (lit (int 5)) (ctor Nil)))))
      (seq
       ; the empty list on the left: `Nil` is Data with ZERO fields, so the tuple arm would
       ; have turned this one into a Tuple1 rather than a Tuple4
       (prim __autoOutput__
        (prim sconcat
         (ctor Nil)
         (ctor Cons (lit (int 7)) (ctor Nil))))
       (seq
        ; ANTI-ROW: strings still concatenate as strings
        (prim __autoOutput__
         (prim sconcat (lit (str "ab")) (lit (str "cd"))))
        ; ANTI-ROW: two tuples still concatenate their fields, which is what the Data arm is for
        (prim __autoOutput__
         (prim sconcat
          (ctor Tuple2 (lit (int 1)) (lit (int 2)))
          (ctor Tuple2 (lit (int 3)) (lit (int 4))))))))))))
 (entry (app (global main))))
